1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,800
Sensitive work should not require handing a model your credentials.

2
00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:09,900
CipherVault AI is a browser-local demonstration of a privacy-oriented AI gateway.

3
00:00:09,900 --> 00:00:17,900
It inspects selected sensitive values, replaces them with tokens, and makes policy decisions before a potential model boundary.

4
00:00:17,900 --> 00:00:22,000
This walkthrough shows the real interface, using synthetic data.

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:24,500
Start with a familiar workflow.

6
00:00:24,500 --> 00:00:29,700
A developer enters an example API key and asks how to rotate it safely.

7
00:00:29,700 --> 00:00:32,000
The key is fake test data.

8
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:35,400
CipherVault keeps the prompt in the current browser tab.

9
00:00:35,400 --> 00:00:40,200
A real external model is not connected, so this prompt is not sent to a provider.

10
00:00:40,960 --> 00:00:49,960
The local detector checks a limited set of patterns, including credentials, access tokens, database URLs, contact details, and custom phrases.

11
00:00:49,960 --> 00:00:58,060
When it finds a match, the value is encrypted in memory and replaced with an ordered placeholder such as Secret underscore zero zero one.

12
00:00:58,060 --> 00:01:03,660
The preview panel shows sanitized context that could be used across a future model boundary.

13
00:01:03,660 --> 00:01:09,960
Instead of making a network call, the prototype returns a clearly labeled canned demonstration response.

14
00:01:09,960 --> 00:01:16,360
The original prompt remains visible to you, but the sanitized payload trace excludes the fake credential.

15
00:01:16,360 --> 00:01:23,760
The dashboard separately reports zero provider transmissions because the external model connection is intentionally off.

16
00:01:24,380 --> 00:01:27,780
Open the local vault view to see the key-isolation concept.

17
00:01:27,780 --> 00:01:35,380
Browser Web Crypto creates a non-exportable AES GCM key, and encrypted entries live only in this tab's memory.

18
00:01:35,380 --> 00:01:38,580
Clearing them or refreshing ends the demo state.

19
00:01:38,580 --> 00:01:44,680
This is not a hardware enclave, a hardened vault, or a guarantee of physical memory zeroization.

20
00:01:44,680 --> 00:01:48,880
The attack lab runs eight curated local policy fixtures.

21
00:01:48,880 --> 00:01:57,580
These include direct requests for a master key, arbitrary secrets, vault export, environment variables, and attempts to override instructions.

22
00:01:57,580 --> 00:02:00,580
The result is limited to this selected fixture set.

23
00:02:00,580 --> 00:02:04,580
It is not an exhaustive security test or proof against unknown attacks.

24
00:02:05,180 --> 00:02:12,180
Security events record the rule category, timestamp, and outcome without including the original prompt or detected value.

25
00:02:12,180 --> 00:02:15,480
The flow panel makes the intended boundary visible.

26
00:02:15,480 --> 00:02:23,680
User context enters CipherVault, the policy layer evaluates it, and only approved sanitized context could reach a future model.

27
00:02:23,680 --> 00:02:26,580
In this prototype, that model step remains off.

28
00:02:26,580 --> 00:02:35,780
CipherVault does not claim that an AI sees no data, that arbitrary text can be hidden while it is understood, or that any system is impossible to hack.

29
00:02:35,780 --> 00:02:39,480
Pattern matching is incomplete and policy coverage is limited.

30
00:02:39,480 --> 00:02:44,980
A real gateway needs server-side key custody, hardened controls, and independent review.

31
00:02:44,980 --> 00:02:49,480
CipherVault AI: useful context, no key access.

32
00:02:50,080 --> 00:02:51,580
Use synthetic data only.

33
00:02:51,580 --> 00:02:53,780
Inspect every decision carefully.

34
00:02:53,780 --> 00:02:58,080
And remember: this prototype is not a security certification.
